Task 7 – Understanding User Preferences and Preparing Interaction Data



1. A simulated interaction dataset was created with 8 users and up to 10 TED Talks selected from the cleaned TED Talks dataset.
2. Each user interacted with a different selection of TED Talks.
3. Ratings were generated between 1 and 5 to represent user preferences.
4. Users who gave ratings of 4 or 5 were considered to like the TED Talk.
5. Users who gave ratings of 1 or 2 were considered to dislike the TED Talk.
6. The user-item interaction matrix represents the ratings of different users for different TED Talks.
7. Missing values indicate that users have not rated particular TED Talks.
8. The interaction analysis helps identify popular TED Talks and users who interact with more content.
9. Collaborative filtering can use these interaction patterns to identify users with similar interests.
10. User preferences can improve the recommendation system by providing personalized TED Talk recommendations.


